# Every variable this project reads, in one place.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS ONLY — this file is tracked and the repository is public. The
# real one is `.env`, which is gitignored. Never put a working secret here.
#
# Two audiences, marked below:
#   [dev]    what `npm run dev` / `npm test` need on a workstation.
#   [deploy] what docker/docker-compose.yml (or your own compose) needs.
# A variable with no marker is needed by both.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [dev] The API and the migration runner read this directly. `web` must NEVER
# receive it (architectural rule 1: web talks to the API over HTTP only) — so
# if you use `env_file:` in compose, point it at the api and migrate services
# only, never at web.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://learn:password@localhost:5432/learn_dev

# [dev] The test suite and the Playwright harness. Separate database: the
# suite drops and recreates tables, and tools/src/e2e-seed.ts refuses to run
# against any database whose name does not say "test".
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://learn:password@localhost:5432/learn_test

# [deploy] These configure the postgres container AND are substituted into the
# DATABASE_URL that compose hands the api. The password here and the one
# inside DATABASE_URL must be THE SAME STRING — drift between them is a
# connection failure that reads like a database outage.
POSTGRES_USER=learn
POSTGRES_DB=learn
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

API_PORT=3001

# WHICH HOP the API believes `X-Forwarded-For` from. This decides what
# `request.ip` is, and the rate limiters (login §13, public profiles §11) key
# on it — so both of the obvious answers are wrong behind a proxy:
#
#   false  Every request appears to come from the proxy, so all callers share
#          ONE bucket. Five failed logins then lock out every user, renewably.
#   true   Trusts every hop, so a client can forge X-Forwarded-For and hand
#          itself a fresh bucket per request. The limit stops existing.
#
# Name the hop instead. Accepted values:
#
#   false            trust nothing. Correct ONLY if the API is exposed directly.
#   private          trust loopback + private ranges. The right answer for the
#                    compose stack, where Caddy and web share a private network.
#   1                trust exactly one hop, when the proxy count is known.
#   10.0.0.0/8,...   an explicit CIDR / address list. The most precise.
#
# Local development runs the API directly, so `false` is correct here.
# docker/docker-compose.yml sets `private`.
API_TRUST_PROXY=false

# HOW MANY REQUESTS PER MINUTE ONE ADDRESS CAN READ A PUBLIC PROFILE. GET
# /api/v1/profiles/:handle is unauthenticated and database-backed, so everyone behind
# one NAT, one office egress, or one misconfigured proxy (see API_TRUST_PROXY) shares
# one bucket for this PUBLIC page. A busy office can exhaust 60/min through ordinary
# browsing, then gets 429 for a minute.
#
# DEFAULT: 60. Unset means byte-identical to before this variable existed.
#
# WHY "OFF" IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE. This endpoint has no account to key on — that is the
# point — so the per-IP bucket is the ONLY thing standing between a scraper and an
# enumeration of every handle (design §11). 0, -1, "off", "none", or too many digits
# are each refused BY NAME at boot, where an operator is watching. A typo that silently
# disabled rate-limiting would surface weeks later in an access log nobody reads.
#
# VALID RANGE: 1 to 100000. Below roughly 10/min, ordinary browsing (one profile view
# is more than one request) gets refused with 429. Above roughly 600 (see
# LOUD_PROFILE_RATE_LIMIT in api/src/auth/profile-rate-limit.ts), it is a test harness
# or trusted network choice rather than a production one, and the boot log says so.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: unset this (or leave it blank). The API runs directly, so you hit
# the default of 60/min — no trouble in normal work. CI's Playwright suite is one
# address making hundreds of requests per minute, so playwright.config.ts sets
# API_PROFILE_RATE_LIMIT=5000 for its test server only.
#
# Cross-reference: API_TRUST_PROXY — both limiters key on request.ip.
API_PROFILE_RATE_LIMIT=

# THE TOKEN SIGNING KEY. Leave it unset and the API mints a THROWAWAY Ed25519
# keypair at boot and says so loudly in the log: every session dies on
# restart, and a second instance rejects the first one's tokens. Fine for
# local development, never for a deployment.
#
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out signing.pem
#   AUTH_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY="$(base64 -w0 signing.pem)"
#
# A PEM block or base64 of one is accepted. Base64 is the practical choice
# here: a multi-line PEM in a .env file is a source of quoting bugs.
#
# AUTH_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY is optional and deliberately omitted — it is derived
# from the private half. Set it only if something else needs to VERIFY tokens
# without being able to sign them.
AUTH_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [deploy] Host port only; compose maps it to the container's 3000.
WEB_PORT=3000

# WHERE WEB REACHES THE API — server-to-server, never from the browser (no
# client component reads this), so in compose it is the API's INTERNAL
# service name, not a public URL.
#
# INLINED AT BUILD TIME. `next build` statically replaces every
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* reference, server code included, so supplying this only under
# `environment:` to a running container does nothing: it must be a build
# `arg`. See docker/web.Dockerfile. `apiBase()` throws when it is unset, so a
# missed build arg fails fast rather than silently calling the wrong host.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
